WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.360
Contrary to some of the other algorithms random&nbsp;
forest is actually fairly easy to understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.200
Random forest just merges a collection of&nbsp;
independent decision trees to get a more accurate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.280
and stable prediction.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.760
Random forest is a type of ensemble method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:29.200
Ensemble combines several machine learning models&nbsp;
to decrease both bias and variance. The overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.840
idea here is that multiple models are fit and&nbsp;
each key in the different aspect of the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.520
By combining these models, ultimately generate&nbsp;
better prediction together than any of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.840
single models on their own.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.560
Let's look at a quick example. We will&nbsp;
start with what the training looks like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.960
and then we will look at what&nbsp;
it is like to test the model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.600
In training, this algorithm will take N samples&nbsp;
from the training data. This is sampling with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.440
replacement. That means one single sample is&nbsp;
likely to appear in multiple different samples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.120
Then, the next step is to take a sample of&nbsp;
features to be used for each of the data samples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.640
In the Titanic dataset, if we have 7&nbsp;
features, each of these data samples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.680
maybe we'll sample four or five&nbsp;
of those features to be used.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.720
So now, we have N subsets of our overall data.&nbsp;
And those subsets contain both a subset of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.080
the rows and also a subset of the columns.&nbsp;
Then with the features and the data subset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.240
the algorithm will build a decision tree&nbsp;
and try to generate the most accurate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.680
results. It's important to realize that these&nbsp;
decision trees are all developed on their own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.680
They do not know what the other trees are doing.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.560
In summary, we would have N trees that are all&nbsp;
build independently on different subsets of data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.600
It's important that these trees are all&nbsp;
independent because you want each decision tree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.200
to key in on different relationships in the data.&nbsp;
This will ultimately generate the best prediction.&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.520
Next, let's look at how the&nbsp;
prediction is generated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.640
Each of these tress represent a fit and concrete&nbsp;
model. We would take an example from the test set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.600
let’s call it example A. Example A will enter&nbsp;
into all trees that have been built and traverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.720
down the tree based on its features. Then,&nbsp;
each of the entry will generate a prediction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:26.400
Let’s look at the most left hand side tree. May&nbsp;
be example A stays at the cabin. Then move on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:33.200
the second layer of the tree, may be this person&nbsp;
pay the $50. Then, we move on to the third layer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.120
and may be this person is a female. Based on these&nbsp;
features, this model will predict that this person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.800
would survive.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.400
Now, the splits in the features on the&nbsp;
second tree will be totally different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.640
So, it's possible to get a different prediction.&nbsp;
If we continue the testing, you will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:56.160
N predictions for example A, one from each&nbsp;
tree. Then the random forest algorithm will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.360
basically just aggregate all those predictions&nbsp;
together, and based on voting with majority rule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.640
you will make a final prediction. In this case,&nbsp;
it would predict that the person survived.&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.840
When should you consider using Random Forest?
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.360
Contrary to logistic regression in SVM, random&nbsp;
forest works quite well for problems with both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:23.760
categorical and continuous target variables. One&nbsp;
of the major advantage of random forest is that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.680
has an attribute that lays out the importance&nbsp;
of each predictor that you're working with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.800
This is really helpful to understand the&nbsp;
relationships and the power of the features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.240
that you're using in your model. Hence, random&nbsp;
forest is great as a quick benchmark model.&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.920
&nbsp;
It tends to perform quite well, it's relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:47.920
fast to train, and it's really quite flexible in&nbsp;
the type of data that it plays well with. On that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.760
note, when you have really messy data with missing&nbsp;
values, outliers, and maybe skewed data, random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.960
forest deals really well with that kind of data.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.680
When should we not use this? While random forest&nbsp;
is fairly fast, flexible, and performs quite well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.520
it's not necessarily likely to be&nbsp;
the best model for any given problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.760
If you want a quick model that gets you 90% of the&nbsp;
way there, it might be the right tool for the job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.720
but if you really need to get 100% of the way&nbsp;
there and extract all possible value out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.240
data, random forest might not be the right tool.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.080
While it's nice to understand the features,&nbsp;
and the decision trees on their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.080
can be fairly easy to understand. When you have&nbsp;
a random forest of hundreds of decision trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.520
it can be hard to interpret what's really&nbsp;
happening across that entire model. So, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.160
need to see the details within the model, random&nbsp;
forest might not be the right choice for you.&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:43.920
&nbsp;
In summary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.800
random forest is relatively quick to train, but&nbsp;
it's not quite as quick to make predictions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.640
Overall, random forest is a tremendously&nbsp;
flexible, relatively fast tool that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:05.680
plays well with different data and can&nbsp;
give you relatively good performance.

